xternal vector database configuration.\n- The gen-ai BFF test suite passes in CI (build pipeline green).\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-1 (out-of-the-box default), AC-2 (playground RAG functions), AC-3 (separate process — remote provider, not inline), AC-4 (multi-tenant scoping).\n\nWait — AC-1 \"no manual provisioning\" is delivered jointly with the provisioning branch epic. E002 covers the wiring half. Fine, list both epics in matrix.\n\n**E003 — Implementation: Validate pgvector as the default vector store for Gen AI Studio playground RAG**\n\nHmm, title must avoid enforcement regex — \"Validate pgvector as the default vector store for Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows\" — no match. Good.\n\nDescription: Delivers the strategy's validation evidence that the pgvector-backed default vector store preserves Gen AI Studio playground RAG capability: an end-to-end validation of playground RAG workflows against the provisioned default vector store, a benchmark comparing pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline, per-namespace isolation verification, and a resource-footprint assessment. Per the Staff Engineer Input, the default instance targets development experimentation: no strict latency SLAs — the benchmark verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline, and flat scan (no index) is the expected access path for typical playground document sets. Auto Rag compatibility is out of scope (addressed by RHAISTRAT-7824).\n\nScope:\n- Capture the inline Milvus baseline for representative playground RAG operations (document ingestion, embedding, retrieval) before inline providers are removed.\n- Benchmark the pgvector default vector store on the same operations and compare order of magnitude against the baseline.\n- End-to-end validation of playground RAG workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval-augmented generation) against the default vector store provisioned via the selected provisioning path, verifying no loss of current capability.\n- Verify per-namespace isolation: vector data in one namespace is not visible to another.\n- Record the observed resource footprint of the default vector store and a sizing recommendation (starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits), confirming it is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments.\n\nACs:\n- A benchmark report exists comparing inline Milvus baseline and pgvector results for representative playground RAG operations, showing pgvector within the same order of magnitude.\n- An end-to-end validation record exists showing playground RAG workflows functioning against the default vector store with no loss of current capability.\n- Isolation validation record: cross-namespace vector data access is not possible.\n- A sizing recommendation document records the observed resource footprint and requests/limits recommendation.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-2, AC-4, AC-5.\n\n**BRANCH-A-E004 — Implementation: Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (path A: RHAISTRAT-7164 lands)**\n\nDescription: Conditional branch plan executed if the go/no-go decision confirms the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) delivers the RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time: implement PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management in that operator layer, following the model-registry-operator pattern, so every Gen AI Studio playground that needs RAG gets a PostgreSQL instance with pgvector provisioned automatically in its namespace.\n\nScope:\n- In the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the dashboard modularization, implement reconciliation of Gen AI Studio PostgreSQL instances: Deployment, Service, PVC, and connection Secret, using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included out of the box — no custom image build, no additional pipeline onboarding).\n- Follow the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioning pattern for the resource set and lifecycle behavior.\n- Set resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n- Define secret ownership: the operator creates and manages the PostgreSQL credentials consumed by the gen-ai BFF when configuring OGX instances.\n- Per-namespace scoping: one PostgreSQL instance per playground namespace.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (via the DataScienceCluster in a later release), Gen AI Studio consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n\nACs:\n- With the operator deployed, creating/configuring a playground with RAG results in a PostgreSQL instance (Deployment, Service, PVC, Secret) with pgvector available in the playground's namespace, without manual user action.\n- Automated tests cover the provisioning and cleanup lifecycle (creation, secret management, deletion with the owning playground).\n- The provisioned instance's resource requests/limits match the documented sizing starting point.\n- The operator's build pipeline and test suite remain green.\n- Disconnected-install support: the PostgreSQL image reference is handled the same way as the existing related-image environment variable pattern used for the model-registry PostgreSQL. (Hmm — SE said RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE already present in rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs. Good AC.)\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove — if RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time, this epic does not exist; fallback = BFF-managed provisioning (the other branch) or bring-your-own PostgreSQL.\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-1, AC-3, AC-4, AC-5 (bundled: sizing on same Deployment).\n\n**BRANCH-B-E004 — Implementation: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (path B: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land)**\n\nDescription: Conditional branch plan executed if the go/no-go decision finds the operator layer unavailable for the release: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, similar to how it already provisions guardrails infrastructure — creating the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret in the playground namespace when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists yet.\n\nScope:\n- In the gen-ai BFF, implement idempotent provisioning: when a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included); reuse an existing instance instead of duplicating.\n- Address lifecycle management explicitly (the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop): cleanup on playground deletion, behavior on pod restarts, and credential handling must be designed and tested.\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (later release), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n- Resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n- Per-namespace scoping: one instance per playground namespace.\n- If, at the gate, even BFF-managed provisioning is rejected, the recorded fallback is bring-your-own PostgreSQL: users configure the OGX instance spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details (documented configuration support).\n\nACs:\n- An automated test demonstrates that configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace without a PostgreSQL instance creates the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret, and that re-configuring does not duplicate the instance.\n- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and tested: playground deletion cleans up the provisioned instance per the documented design; pod restarts and credential handling behave as designed.\n- The provisioning code path is portable: switching to a shared platform connection string requires configuration change only, not reimplementation (verified by test with an injected connection string).\n- The gen-ai BFF test suite passes in CI (build pipeline green).\n\ngate_fail