{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-28265fb2-fff1-484d-b3c5-3ce0c143f83c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b05f94abf6de7e67","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-ww59_x00/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E002.md","content":"# Configure the remote pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground OGX instances\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the Gen AI Studio side of the default vector store: when a playground with RAG capabilities is configured, the gen-ai backend-for-frontend sets the OGX instance's vector I/O provider to the remote pgvector provider, pointed at the PostgreSQL connection available in the playground's namespace. This replaces reliance on the inline Milvus provider that RHAISTRAT-7474 removes from the OGX distribution, keeping playground RAG working out of the box. Per the Staff Engineer Input, the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; the work is the configuration wiring in the gen-ai BFF code path where OGX instances are created and configured for playgrounds today.\n\n## Scope\n\n- In the gen-ai BFF code path that creates and configures OGX instances for playgrounds, set the vector_io provider configuration to the remote pgvector provider (the distribution's supported pgvector provider activation), using the PostgreSQL connection available in the playground's namespace — regardless of whether that connection is provided by an operator or created by the BFF itself.\n- Scope the configuration per playground namespace (one vector store per namespace), matching the confirmed scoping model.\n- Leave the OGX application storage backend configuration untouched (it remains on its existing default): vector storage and application storage are independent configurations, and no storage migration is in scope.\n- Keep the vector_io provider selection overridable so an external, production-grade vector database can be configured instead of the default — this is both the migration path raised in the strategy's open questions and the rollback path for the replaced inline-provider capability.\n- Update playground documentation references that fall out of this change incidentally (no substantive new content authoring).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- An automated test demonstrates that configuring a playground with RAG results in an OGX instance whose vector_io provider is the remote pgvector provider using the PostgreSQL connection available in the playground's namespace.\n- An automated test demonstrates per-namespace scoping: playgrounds in different namespaces use separate vector store instances.\n- An automated test demonstrates that the OGX application storage backend configuration is unchanged by this work (vector storage configuration only).\n- An automated test demonstrates that the vector_io provider can be overridden to an external vector database configuration (migration/rollback path).\n- The gen-ai BFF test suite passes and the build pipeline remains green.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): delivers the wiring that makes the provisioned PostgreSQL the playground's default vector store.\n- AC-2 (playground RAG workflows function with no loss of capability): the wired provider is what the playground RAG workflows run against; capability evidence is completed by the validation epic.\n- AC-3 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance): the remote pgvector provider runs outside the OGX process, unlike the inline provider it replaces.\n- AC-4 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace configuration of the vector store.\n- SC-1 (no functional regression when inline providers are removed): the replacement configuration this epic delivers.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":79986,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},