{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be58c56e22de123","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: 'BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (path B: RHAISTRAT-7164\n4\t  does not land)'\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: Executed only if the go/no-go decision finds the RHAISTRAT-7164\n17\t    operator layer unavailable for the release. If the operator path is selected instead,\n18\t    this epic is removed in favor of the operator-based provisioning branch. If even\n19\t    BFF-managed provisioning is rejected at the gate, the recorded fallback is bring-your-own\n20\t    PostgreSQL (documented OGX configuration support, no provisioning code).\n21\tai_signals:\n22\t  change_specificity: 1\n23\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n24\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n25\t  existing_foundation: 1\n26\t  open_questions: 0\n27\t  external_dependency: 0\n28\t  human_process_gates: 0\n29\t  repo_access: 1\n30\t  architecture_claims: 1\n31\timplementation_type: null\n32\tinvestigation_signals: null\n33\tai_implementability: High\n34\tai_implementability_score: 5\n35\tjira_key: null\n36\t---\n37\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio (path B: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land)\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tConditional branch plan, executed if the go/no-go decision finds the RHOAI-controlled operator layer unavailable for the release: the gen-ai backend-for-frontend provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, similar to how it already provisions guardrails infrastructure — creating the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret in the playground namespace when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists yet. This keeps the out-of-the-box RAG experience without waiting for the operator layer. If, at the gate, even BFF-managed provisioning is rejected, the recorded fallback is bring-your-own PostgreSQL: users configure the OGX instance with their own PostgreSQL connection details (documented configuration support only, no provisioning code).\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- In the gen-ai BFF, implement idempotent provisioning: when a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image, which includes pgvector out of the box (no custom image build); reuse an existing instance instead of duplicating it.\n46\t- Address lifecycle management explicitly — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, behavior on pod restarts, and credential handling must be designed, implemented, and tested.\n47\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DataScienceCluster becomes available (a later release), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning — switching must require configuration change only.\n48\t- Scope provisioning per playground namespace: one PostgreSQL instance per namespace.\n49\t- Set resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- An automated test demonstrates that configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace without a PostgreSQL instance creates the Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret, and that re-configuring does not duplicate the instance.\n54\t- An integration check demonstrates a playground RAG workflow functioning against an OGX instance whose vector_io provider uses the BFF-provisioned PostgreSQL.\n55\t- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and tested: playground deletion cleans up the provisioned instance per the documented design, and pod restarts and credential handling behave as designed.\n56\t- Portability is verified by test: switching to a shared platform connection string requires a configuration change only, not reimplementation.\n57\t- The gen-ai BFF test suite passes and the build pipeline remains green.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- AC-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning): the provisioning this epic delivers.\n62\t- AC-3 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance): PostgreSQL runs as its own deployment, outside the OGX process.\n63\t- AC-4 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provisioning.\n64\t- AC-5 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments, bundled): resource requests/limits are set on the same Deployment this epic creates — same surface, not separately schedulable.\n65\t- SC-2 (no additional manual setup steps): automatic provisioning removes manual setup.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e3984cba26ba204","agent_id":"aaec9aa803340dfa3","session_id":"0064224b-16f9-4322-85c9-ccae87fefaaf","uuid":"3180d60a-6793-430c-887a-a219405f4437","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:40:24.513Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be58c56e22de123","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
