nput rather than the\nrhods-operator context file. BRANCH-A's greenfield signals\n(`existing_foundation: -1`, `repo_access: 0`, `change_specificity: 0`) are\nconsistent with the not-yet-existing operator-layer target.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. E002, as the\nreplacement for the inline Milvus provider, carries the override/rollback AC\n(vector_io provider overridable to an external vector database) which also\ndisposes of the strategy's migration-path open question. Both branch epics\ncarry lifecycle and integration ACs; BRANCH-B additionally carries idempotency\nand portability ACs addressing the Staff Engineer's explicit lifecycle\ncaveats. E003's ACs are report/record deliverables with defined pass/fail\n(order-of-magnitude comparison, not a threshold — matching the\ndev-experimentation scope). No AC states an externally-controlled\nprecondition: E001's ACs are on the decision record the epic produces (the\nowner confirmation is cited evidence, not the deliverable). No epic body\nreferences sibling epics by draft ID — cross-epic coupling is expressed via\nACs and prose descriptions only.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: the six acceptance criteria appear as\nAC-1..AC-5 plus the documented Auto Rag removal; the three success criteria\nare mapped (SC-3 as whole-plan outcome, justified); both strategy open\nquestions are explicitly dispositioned (scoping model resolved by E001/E002\nscope; migration path as E002's override AC); Correction 3's no-migration\nconstraint is carried into E002's scope and testable AC. Staff Engineer\ncontext is carried forward: resource sizing starting point (E003 + both\nbranches), portability to the 3.6 shared platform database (both branches),\nbenchmark-vs-baseline framing (E003), and the RHAISTRAT-7474 unblocking note\n(Health Warnings). Conditional branches cover both bounded outcomes of the\ngate (A: operator path; B: BFF-manag